course: history
lesson: 15
title: Rome: daily life, gladiators, Latin and Christianity
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Rome had about a million people, a huge number for the time. Rich Romans lived in houses with gardens and mosaics, while most people squeezed into blocks of flats called insulae, which could collapse or catch fire.
explain: The town centre was the forum, where people shopped, did business and listened to speeches. Most people also went to the public baths every day, like a swimming pool, gym and café all in one.
explain: For fun, crowds cheered chariot teams at the Circus Maximus, like football fans, and watched gladiators in the Colosseum. Many gladiators were slaves or prisoners, and Roman wealth depended on enslaved people.
explain: Rome left us Latin, the language behind French, Spanish, Italian and about half of English words. The town of Pompeii, buried by a volcano in AD 79, shows Roman life frozen in time. And from Judea, a Roman province, the teaching of Jesus spread along Roman roads.
fact: The forum was the centre of a Roman town, with markets, temples, law courts and speeches.
fact: Rich Romans lived in houses with gardens and mosaics, but most people lived in crowded blocks of flats called insulae, which could collapse or catch fire.
fact: Rome had about a million people, a huge number for the time.
fact: Ordinary Romans ate bread, porridge, olives, cheese and vegetables, and rich Romans held feasts lying on couches, with dishes like dormice and fish sauce.
fact: Public baths had hot, warm and cold pools, exercise areas and places to chat, and most people went every day; the English city of Bath is named after its Roman baths.
fact: Gladiators fought in arenas to entertain crowds; many were slaves or prisoners, some were volunteers, and famous ones were like sports stars.
fact: Not every gladiator fight ended in death, and the thumbs-up or thumbs-down sign may be a modern guess.
fact: The Colosseum in Rome held about 50,000 people and had a canvas roof for shade and hidden lifts to bring animals up into the arena.
fact: The Circus Maximus held perhaps 150,000 fans, who cheered for chariot teams called the Reds, Whites, Greens and Blues.
fact: Many people in Rome were enslaved, captured in war or born to slaves, and worked in houses, fields, mines and workshops; some were freed, and Spartacus led a huge slave revolt.
fact: Latin was the language of Rome; French, Spanish, Italian, Portuguese and Romanian grew from it, and about half of English words come from Latin, often through French.
fact: In AD 79 the volcano Vesuvius buried the town of Pompeii in ash, and it was dug out much later with streets, shops, paintings and even graffiti.
fact: Jesus lived in the Roman province of Judea and was crucified by the Romans, and his followers spread his teaching through the empire; Christians were sometimes persecuted, but the faith kept growing.
exercise: What was the forum? | The centre of a Roman town with markets and courts | A Roman warship with three rows of oars and a bronze ram | A bath with cold pools | A chariot team
exercise: Where did most people in Rome live? | In crowded blocks of flats called insulae | In big houses with gardens, fountains and painted walls | In tents pitched outside the city walls | In caves under the hills
exercise: What dangers did the insulae have? | They could collapse or catch fire | They were too far from the river to fetch any water | They were full of lions | They had no doors
exercise: About how many people lived in Rome? | About a million | About five thousand | About a billion people | About ten thousand
exercise: What did ordinary Romans mostly eat? | Bread, porridge, olives and vegetables | Potatoes and tomatoes with lots of butter | Rice and noodles | Burgers and chips
exercise: How did rich Romans eat at feasts? | Lying on couches | Standing on tables | Sitting on the floor in silence | Walking around the city
exercise: What could you find at Roman public baths? | Hot, warm and cold pools and places to chat | Only a single cold river with no roof and no seats | Chariot races and gladiator fights | A library of printed books
exercise: Which English city is named after its Roman baths? | Bath | London | York in the north | Manchester
exercise: Who were most gladiators? | Slaves or prisoners, with some volunteers | Roman senators who wanted to be famous and loved | Emperors in disguise | Priests of Jupiter
exercise: About how many people did the Colosseum hold? | About 50,000 | About 500 | About 5 million | About 5,000
exercise: What did the Colosseum have to bring animals up? | Hidden lifts | Escalators worked by slaves | A river running through it | A tunnel to Africa
exercise: What were the colours of the chariot teams? | Reds, Whites, Greens and Blues | Golds and Silvers, like the medals | Blacks and Yellows | Purples and Oranges
exercise: How did people become enslaved in Rome? | Captured in war or born to slaves | By buying a ticket at the market | By winning a race | By joining the Senate and losing a vote
exercise: Who led a huge slave revolt? | Spartacus | Augustus | Hannibal | Boudica
exercise: Which language grew from Latin? | Spanish | Arabic | German | Chinese
exercise: What buried Pompeii in AD 79? | Ash from the volcano Vesuvius | A great flood | An earthquake and sea wave | A sandstorm
exercise: In which Roman province did Jesus live? | Judea | Gaul in the west | Britain | Egypt by the Nile
test: in Gladiator everyone dies in every fight. was it like that? | No, not every fight ended in death | Yes, every single fight | Yes, and the crowd always voted death | No, nobody was ever hurt
test: Thumbs down meant kill, right? Everyone knows that. | Not sure; that sign may be a modern guess | Yes, it is written down clearly in Roman law books | Yes, Caesar invented it | No, it meant "well done"
test: Why does Pompeii tell us so much about Roman life? | Ash buried it quickly, keeping streets, shops and graffiti | The Romans wrote a long guidebook to the town for visitors | It was never part of Rome | People still live in the old houses today
test: Your mate says English has nothing to do with Latin. What is true? | About half of English words come from Latin | No English word at all comes from Latin, not one | English is the same as Latin | Latin came from English
test: Why might Christianity spread so well through the Roman Empire? | Roman roads and one big empire helped people travel | The emperors were all Christian right from the very start | It was only taught in Judea | Everyone was forced at once
test: If the bathhouse is shut for a week, what would Romans miss? | Washing, exercise and chatting with friends | Their only meals of the day, cooked in the pools | Their chariot races and gladiator shows | School lessons
test: Fans scream for the Greens at the Circus Maximus. What is that most like today? | Football supporters cheering their team | A quiet library | A church service | A school exam
test: Why was a fire in the insulae so dangerous? | Many people lived crowded together in tall blocks | The flats were made of ice and melted in the heat | Nobody lived there | Firefighters were not allowed in by law
test: dormice for dinner?? who ate that lol | Rich Romans at their feasts | Every poor Roman each day | Gladiators only | Nobody, it is a joke
test: Roman wealth depended on unfree people. Which shows that? | Enslaved people worked houses, fields and mines | Everyone in Rome was paid well for every job they did | Slavery was banned in Rome | Only animals did any heavy work
test: Which is true about a famous gladiator? | He could be as popular as a sports star | He was always a senator | He never fought anyone | He was paid by Egypt
exam: Identify the volcanic eruption whereby the town of Pompeii was interred in the year 79. | The eruption of Vesuvius | The eruption of Etna | The eruption of Krakatoa | The eruption of Hekla
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dwellings of the majority of the population of Rome? | Crowded tenement blocks termed insulae | Spacious villas with gardens and mosaics | Tents disposed outside the walls | Caverns beneath the seven hills
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the derivation of the English vocabulary. | Approximately one half derives from Latin, frequently through French | No part whatsoever derives from Latin | The whole derives from Greek, by way of the Roman poets | Latin derives from English
exam: Ascertain the origin of the greater part of the gladiators. | Enslaved persons and prisoners, together with some volunteers | Members of the Senate | Officers of the legions | Priests of the temples, chosen by lot each year in the great forum
